<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lodi dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lodi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lodi dynasty was an Afghan dynasty that ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> from 1451 to 1526, the last of its ruling houses. Its final sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>, was defeated and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, a Timurid prince, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> in 1526. This victory ended the Delhi Sultanate and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty.jpg" alt="Lodi dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maps created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan; Territorial area: पाटलिपुत्र, per Schwartberg Atlas p.148 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Lodis.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Lodis were an Afghan or Turco-Afghan tribe, first settled between the Gomal River and the Sulaiman Mountains, who took part in the valuable horse trade between Turan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Bahlul's grandfather, Malik Bahram, was a horse merchant who entered the service of the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> under Firuz Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a>. His son Sultan Shah received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a> and the title of Islam Khan, and gathered a large following there. After Islam Khan's death in 1431 the Lodis split into camps, and Bahlul soon succeeded in uniting them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bahlul Khan Lodi took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 19 April 1451 and reigned until 1489, replacing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-dynasty">Sayyid dynasty</a>. Most of his reign was spent fighting the Sharqi dynasty of the Jaunpur Sultanate, which he annexed, placing his eldest surviving son Barbak on its throne in 1486. His son Sikandar Khan Lodi (r. 1489–1517) refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> as a Muslim city in 1504 and shifted the capital there from Delhi. He required his Pashtun nobles to submit their accounts to a state audit, and conquered and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> from the Sharqis.</p><p>Ibrahim Khan Lodi (r. 1517–1526), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a>'s eldest son, faced numerous rebellions, among them that of his brother Jalaluddin and of Pashtun nobles who backed the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. He arrested the nobles who opposed him.</p><p>Daulat Khan, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, the Timurid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, to invade, and Ibrahim's uncle Alam Khan also supported Babur. At Panipat in April 1526 Babur, with about 24,000 men armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and artillery, faced Ibrahim's army of about 100,000 men and 1,000 elephants, which had never fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons. Ibrahim died on the field with some 20,000 of his men. The remaining Lodi lands passed to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, though Ibrahim's brother Mahmud Lodi kept up resistance until the battle of Ghaghra in 1529.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Like earlier sultans, the Lodis styled themselves deputies of the Abbasid caliphs. They gave cash stipends and revenue-free lands to the Muslim ulama and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shaikhs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a>'s policy was strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy: he destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples and, under pressure from the ulama, allowed the execution of a Brahman. Muslims paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a> and non-Muslims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, though Hindu officers served in the revenue administration. The Lodis minted no gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins for lack of the metals, and taxes were often paid in grain. Lodi architecture made extensive use of domes, and Sikandar composed Persian poetry under the pen name Gulrukhi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lodi dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The independent sultanate ended in the struggle for northern India that followed the fall of the <b>Lodi dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Five successive dynasties, the Mamluk, Khalji, Tughlaq, Sayyid and <b>Lodi</b>, governed the sultanate across 320 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of <b>Lodi</b> of 1454 then stabilised Italian rivalries for forty years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…sack of Delhi left the Delhi Sultanate permanently weakened, a diminished state later ruled by the Sayyid and <b>Lodi</b> dynasties until the Mughal conquest; the campaigns in Persia and Iraq depopulated agricultural regions that had supported dense populations for millennia; and the victory at Ankara temporarily relieved Byzantium,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The battle ended the <b>Lodi dynasty</b> and began Mughal rule in India.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Signed in the city of <b>Lodi</b>, the peace agreement ended the Wars in Lombardy between the Venetian Republic and the Duchy of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
